@incollection{OSullivan2010,
annote = {Scalar field: independent of coordinate system. vector field: magnitude and direction. not independent of coordinate system. (slope is a vector field of the scalar field elevation.)

modeling and storing field data: field data has to come from sampling the real surface and then interpolating.

Methods for storing field data 
1) digitized contours, 
2) mathematical functions, Mathematical function can interpolate (honoring all the control points) or approximate (not strictly honoring) the surface.
3) point systems, 
4) triangulated irregular networks (TINs), and 
5) digital elevation matrices (DEMs).

-Level of accuracy desired depends on subsequent use of interpolated data
-Interpolation requires autocorrelation

Interp approaches
1) Proximity polygons - simple approach. not continuous. useful for "nominal" data like soil type. 
2) Spatial avgs - what is "near"? within radius distance? X nearest neighbors?
3) Inverse distance weighting. Tests for coincidence. If prediction location is exactly coincident with an interp point, use that interp control point as the prediction. ALSO: cannot predict values lower than the minimum or higher than the maximum. ALSO: can raise the distance weight denominator to an exponent. higher values of k {\textgreater} bulls-eyes; values of k 
Which interp method is best? How tell?
- do they look reasonable
- hold back a subset and perform cross-validation
- leave one out cross-validation (for selecting k)

Even more options:
1) bicubic spline fitting: smoothest possible curves (in 2D) that can be fitted and still honor all the data.
2) multiquadric analysis. centers a varying-size circular cone on each of n control points. value of Z at any point is expressed as sum of quadratic cone surfaces.
3) TIN = basically distance-weighted interp

for grids, key slope calculation assumption: grid is dense enough for inclined plane assumption to be reasonable
watershed delineation. Possible directions of flow: rook's case, queen's case. Some good discussion of limitations of drainage network detection in here. 
cool viewshed examples

these are all DETERMINISTIC interpolation methods: one surface solution is possible after method/parameters selected},
author = {O'Sullivan, David and Unwin, David J},
booktitle = {Geographic Information Analysis},
chapter = {9},
pages = {239--276},
publisher = {John Wiley {\&} Sons, Inc.},
title = {{Describing and Analyzing Fields}},
year = {2010}
}
