@incollection{OSullivan2010b,
annote = {"bad news" and "good news"

Bad News:
Spatial autocorrelation: close datapoints more likely to be similar than far datapoints. Violates random sample rule.

First-order spatial variation: variation due to changes in underlying environmental properties
Second-order spatial variation: variation due to interaction effects between observations

MAUP: arbitrary aggregation of higher-resolution data means statistics could just be artefacts of aggregation unit. Can it be turned into an exploratory tool, like autocorrelation? Maybe, with detailed data.

Ecological fallacy: assuming that correlation in space means causation. Or? assuming that statistical relationships at one scale hold at another scale.

Non-uniform space and Edge Effects: "clusters" could be due to some variable unrelated to your research interest. crimes happen where the people are, etc. Edge effects: neighbors only in one direction.

Good News: distance, adjacency, interaction, neighborhood. Examples of each

Summarize "ggod news" relationships in matrices

Proximity polygons, triangulation},
author = {O'Sullivan, David and Unwin, David J},
booktitle = {Geographic Information Analysis},
chapter = {1},
pages = {33--53},
publisher = {John Wiley {\&} Sons, Inc.},
title = {{The Pitfalls and Potential of Spatial Data}},
year = {2010}
}
