@article{Voss2006,
annote = {social sciences need better spatial autocorrelation methods in their exploratory statistics

Autocorrelated residuals
"Common in standard regression analyses of dependent variables that are themselves autocorrelated"

Mechanisms that can cause spatial autocorrelation
-Feedback. Neighbors influence each other.
-Grouping forces, like local housing or labor markets. (When such a variable is responsible for spatial autocorrelation it may be possible to identify the variable(s) involved and operationalize them)
-Grouping responses (individuals responding the same way to forces)
- nuisance autocorrelation, distinct from "substantive" or mechanism-based autocorrelation. most common: undelying spatial process creates regions of attribute clustering that are much larger than the units of observation chosen/available to the analyst. Makes spatial autocorrelation inevitable.

Why do autocorrelated residuals cause problems? violates the independence assumption. Makes statistical inference unreliable because 1) estimated regression parameters are biased and inconisstent, or 2) standard errors of the parameter estmitaes are baised. 
Basically, positively autocorrelated residuals may lead to standard errors that are too small, which could mean you claim statistical significance when it is unwarranted. 

How do we deal with autocorrelation?
1) consider removing large-scale drift / spatial dependence. A pre-analysis step because spatial regression assumes a stationary spatial process.
2) fit standard OLS model
3) with a theory about why autocorrelation is present, and using OLS diagnostics, fit a spatial regression model. 
4) Maybe repeat from step 1. 

Reanalysis:
1) test dependent variable for normality. Test the bivariate relationship of each indep var with the dep var for linearity.
2) Moran scatterplot (identify and possibly exclude outliers) and "local" Moran's I map
3) spatial error model. Wu = "Wu is a n {\textperiodcentered}1 vector of spatial lags of the model disturbance term u"??

Explaining spatial autocorrelation: portions may be "explained" bay independent variables (which are autocorrelated). Remainder assued to occur because of spatial autocorrelation among disturbance terms, which happens because one or more relevant autocorr. variable is omitted from the design matrix.

spatial lag model... does not perform as well as the spatial error model. Much of the variation appears unaccounted for/summarized in the error term


Linear regression assumption:
Residuals are not correlated. Violated if they're autocorrelated.},
author = {Voss, Paul R and Long, David D and Hammer, Roger B},
doi = {10.1007/s11113-006-9007-4},
isbn = {1111300690},
journal = {Population Research Policy Review},
number = {November},
pages = {369--391},
title = {{County child poverty rates in the US : a spatial regression approach}},
volume = {25},
year = {2006}
}
